--TEST--
The six truthiness positions are the whole list: a binding, an argument, a property, a return and an array element still want `as bool`, and a `match (true)` label is compared rather than tested — `rule:expressions/truthy-positions`, `rule:expressions/switch-match-equality`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:types/truthiness
// `rule:expressions/truthy-positions` closes its list at six, and the other
// half of that sentence is what this case pins: every `bool` position outside
// the list is an ordinary assignment, refused the way any other type mismatch
// is. One `string` reaches five of them here, so a position that quietly grew
// the exception loses its error.
//
// The `match (true)` arm is the last line for the same reason. It is not a
// sixth-and-a-half position: `rule:expressions/switch-match-equality` compares
// each arm against the subject, so a `string` arm against a `bool` subject is
// `rule:expressions/disjoint-comparison-refused`'s error rather than a test.
class Gate {
    public bool $open = false;

    public static function take(bool $flag): string {
        if ($flag) {
            return "open";
        }
        return "shut";
    }

    public static function asked(string $name): bool {
        return $name;
    }
}

string $name = "ada";

bool $stored = $name;

echo Gate::take($name), "\n";

Gate $gate = new Gate();
$gate->open = $name;

array<bool> $flags = [$name];

echo match (true) {
    $name => "tested",
    default => "compared",
}, "\n";
--EXPECT--

